WEBVTT

0
00:00.240 --> 00:01.810
<v Krista>Before we get into all the other things</v>

1
00:01.810 --> 00:03.750
we're going to talk about in this course,

2
00:03.750 --> 00:06.930
I want to talk a little bit about numbers themselves,

3
00:06.930 --> 00:09.010
actually just numbers,

4
00:09.010 --> 00:12.180
because there's more to numbers than we think about

5
00:12.180 --> 00:14.610
when we just think about numbers in our daily lives.

6
00:14.610 --> 00:17.090
When you think about numbers in general,

7
00:17.090 --> 00:19.890
you tend to think about these kinds of numbers,

8
00:19.890 --> 00:22.940
the counting numbers, so one, two,

9
00:22.940 --> 00:27.100
three, four, five, and on forever.

10
00:27.100 --> 00:29.690
We're used to seeing those kinds of numbers all the time.

11
00:29.690 --> 00:32.150
We deal with those in real life.

12
00:32.150 --> 00:34.670
And sometimes people will also think about

13
00:34.670 --> 00:38.620
this set of numbers but also including the number zero

14
00:38.620 --> 00:41.290
because, of course, zero is a number as well.

15
00:41.290 --> 00:43.200
Either way, these are the numbers

16
00:43.200 --> 00:45.150
that we tend to think about,

17
00:45.150 --> 00:46.980
that we use in our daily lives,

18
00:46.980 --> 00:51.000
but there are a lot of other different kinds of numbers

19
00:51.000 --> 00:52.830
that we want to be familiar with

20
00:52.830 --> 00:54.090
as we're going through this course.

21
00:54.090 --> 00:56.480
And we're going to go into all these other numbers

22
00:56.480 --> 01:00.500
in a lot more detail, so this is really just a preview

23
01:00.500 --> 01:02.970
of the kinds of numbers that you'll see

24
01:02.970 --> 01:04.280
throughout the course.

25
01:04.280 --> 01:07.260
You don't have to understand all of the detail about them,

26
01:07.260 --> 01:09.740
but I want to sort of give you an introduction

27
01:09.740 --> 01:12.430
to the different kinds of numbers that we'll deal with.

28
01:12.430 --> 01:16.040
So, these numbers here, zero, one, two, three, four, five,

29
01:16.040 --> 01:17.930
these are called whole numbers.

30
01:17.930 --> 01:19.600
So, let's go ahead, we're going to build ourselves

31
01:19.600 --> 01:21.490
a little system down here.

32
01:21.490 --> 01:25.873
Let's go ahead and write this as whole numbers,

33
01:28.220 --> 01:33.220
and those include all of these positive numbers plus zero.

34
01:33.860 --> 01:36.060
So, zero, one,

35
01:36.060 --> 01:39.380
two, three, four, five,

36
01:39.380 --> 01:42.520
and of course we could keep going on forever.

37
01:42.520 --> 01:44.130
These are the whole numbers.

38
01:44.130 --> 01:47.420
So, we define an even narrower set of numbers

39
01:47.420 --> 01:50.473
inside of whole numbers that's called natural numbers.

40
01:52.130 --> 01:53.290
Natural numbers

41
01:54.780 --> 01:57.270
are all of these same numbers,

42
01:57.270 --> 01:59.180
everything except the zero,

43
01:59.180 --> 02:03.940
so one, two, three, four, five,

44
02:03.940 --> 02:06.400
and continuing on forever.

45
02:06.400 --> 02:08.830
We also sometimes call these natural numbers

46
02:09.980 --> 02:13.340
counting numbers, and of course that makes a lot of sense

47
02:13.340 --> 02:14.920
because when we learn to count,

48
02:14.920 --> 02:16.690
we learn to count starting with one

49
02:16.690 --> 02:20.300
as one, two, three, four, five, all the way up to 10.

50
02:20.300 --> 02:22.810
That's how we learn to count even when we're little

51
02:22.810 --> 02:25.940
and so sometimes we call those counting numbers.

52
02:25.940 --> 02:29.170
But then if you add to that natural number set,

53
02:29.170 --> 02:30.650
and all of these that we're talking about here

54
02:30.650 --> 02:32.140
are called number sets,

55
02:32.140 --> 02:35.840
so if you add to the set of natural numbers the number zero,

56
02:35.840 --> 02:38.550
then you have the set of whole numbers.

57
02:38.550 --> 02:41.830
We're also going to talk about in this course negative numbers,

58
02:41.830 --> 02:44.020
so all of these same positive numbers

59
02:44.020 --> 02:46.740
except they're negative versions.

60
02:46.740 --> 02:49.640
And again, don't worry too much about the details of this

61
02:49.640 --> 02:51.910
'cause we're going to go over all of it.

62
02:51.910 --> 02:54.330
But if we add in negative numbers,

63
02:54.330 --> 02:58.930
for instance, negative three, negative two, negative one,

64
02:58.930 --> 03:01.170
but we keep all these same values that we had,

65
03:01.170 --> 03:02.740
so we're still including zero,

66
03:02.740 --> 03:04.860
we're still including all these positive numbers,

67
03:04.860 --> 03:09.270
zero, one, two, three, and on forever,

68
03:09.270 --> 03:10.670
four, five, six, et cetera,

69
03:10.670 --> 03:13.090
and even on this way forever,

70
03:13.090 --> 03:16.050
negative four, negative five, negative six, negative seven,

71
03:16.050 --> 03:17.550
we keep going that way,

72
03:17.550 --> 03:21.750
these are called the integers.

73
03:21.750 --> 03:25.790
So, the integers are just all of the whole numbers,

74
03:25.790 --> 03:29.110
but in addition to that, the negative numbers included,

75
03:29.110 --> 03:32.310
the negative versions of these positive whole numbers.

76
03:32.310 --> 03:34.590
When we start including those negative numbers,

77
03:34.590 --> 03:37.680
this set of numbers we call integers.

78
03:37.680 --> 03:40.690
And then an even broader number set

79
03:40.690 --> 03:43.860
that includes even more values than the integers

80
03:43.860 --> 03:44.970
are what we call

81
03:46.850 --> 03:48.123
rational numbers.

82
03:51.920 --> 03:54.160
And rational numbers are going to include

83
03:54.160 --> 03:56.540
all of these numbers that we had in integers,

84
03:56.540 --> 03:58.260
which of course include all the whole numbers,

85
03:58.260 --> 04:00.020
which include all the natural numbers.

86
04:00.020 --> 04:02.620
It's going to include all of the integers,

87
04:02.620 --> 04:05.180
but it's also going to include fractions.

88
04:05.180 --> 04:07.280
And we have several sections in the course

89
04:07.280 --> 04:08.300
devoted to fractions,

90
04:08.300 --> 04:10.420
so we're going to go over these in more detail,

91
04:10.420 --> 04:15.380
but fractions are things like negative seven over two

92
04:15.380 --> 04:18.470
or three over four

93
04:18.470 --> 04:22.020
or 11 over six, things like that.

94
04:22.020 --> 04:25.440
So, all of these values, but also the integers,

95
04:25.440 --> 04:27.490
so let's just put in some integers there.

96
04:27.490 --> 04:32.490
How about a negative six and a positive 12 here.

97
04:33.510 --> 04:36.060
Those are all rational numbers.

98
04:36.060 --> 04:37.420
And then above those,

99
04:37.420 --> 04:40.530
and this is sort of overarching everything,

100
04:40.530 --> 04:41.650
are what we call

101
04:42.910 --> 04:44.033
real numbers.

102
04:47.090 --> 04:48.680
And because real numbers sort of

103
04:48.680 --> 04:52.650
sit over the top of everything and include all of these,

104
04:52.650 --> 04:55.330
we describe this whole number system,

105
04:55.330 --> 04:57.520
all these numbers that we work with

106
04:57.520 --> 04:59.140
as the real number system.

107
04:59.140 --> 05:01.080
So, when we put them all together,

108
05:01.080 --> 05:03.240
we talk about the real number system

109
05:03.240 --> 05:05.640
and it includes all of these numbers

110
05:05.640 --> 05:06.830
that we're going to deal with.

111
05:06.830 --> 05:08.610
So you can kind of think about it this way.

112
05:08.610 --> 05:10.230
We have real numbers at the top

113
05:10.230 --> 05:14.000
and then that is going to include rational numbers,

114
05:14.000 --> 05:16.170
rational numbers includes integers,

115
05:16.170 --> 05:18.490
integers includes whole numbers,

116
05:18.490 --> 05:22.330
whole numbers includes natural or counting numbers.

117
05:22.330 --> 05:24.220
So, each one of these number systems

118
05:24.220 --> 05:26.890
includes all of the number systems below it.

119
05:26.890 --> 05:29.470
And then over here on this other side,

120
05:29.470 --> 05:32.270
also included in the real number system

121
05:32.270 --> 05:35.333
are what we call irrational numbers.

122
05:37.620 --> 05:39.480
And if we try to remember this diagram,

123
05:39.480 --> 05:40.670
that should make sense to us

124
05:40.670 --> 05:42.970
because we have rational over here on the left

125
05:42.970 --> 05:44.990
and irrational over here on the right,

126
05:44.990 --> 05:46.090
so they go together.

127
05:46.090 --> 05:48.570
Rational and irrational numbers

128
05:48.570 --> 05:51.210
are sort of opposite number systems.

129
05:51.210 --> 05:53.270
All of these things over here on the left

130
05:53.270 --> 05:55.220
are part of the rational number system,

131
05:55.220 --> 05:57.540
and then over here, totally separately,

132
05:57.540 --> 05:59.450
we have irrational numbers.

133
05:59.450 --> 06:01.680
And remember that over here with rational numbers

134
06:01.680 --> 06:03.810
we talked about including fractions,

135
06:03.810 --> 06:05.380
which again we'll talk about more later,

136
06:05.380 --> 06:08.020
but fractions were these things like negative seven halves,

137
06:08.020 --> 06:13.020
three fourths, 11 over six, all of these kinds of values.

138
06:13.070 --> 06:15.500
Irrational numbers, all you need to know right now

139
06:15.500 --> 06:18.410
is that irrational numbers are values

140
06:18.410 --> 06:21.150
that cannot be written as fractions.

141
06:21.150 --> 06:24.290
So, all of these numbers over here on the left,

142
06:24.290 --> 06:28.480
one way or another, we can express them as a fraction,

143
06:28.480 --> 06:30.000
but there are a few numbers

144
06:30.000 --> 06:32.600
that we can't write as a fraction.

145
06:32.600 --> 06:35.440
They can't be defined in fraction form,

146
06:35.440 --> 06:37.870
and those are numbers like, for instance,

147
06:37.870 --> 06:40.290
the square root of two,

148
06:40.290 --> 06:42.770
and we're going to talk about square roots and roots

149
06:42.770 --> 06:44.020
later on in this course.

150
06:44.020 --> 06:45.830
So again, you don't need to know what this means,

151
06:45.830 --> 06:48.480
but we're going to talk about what roots are,

152
06:48.480 --> 06:52.460
but the square root of two cannot be written as a fraction.

153
06:52.460 --> 06:54.950
Numbers like pi.

154
06:54.950 --> 06:56.890
Pi is a special number that we use

155
06:56.890 --> 06:59.170
to calculate the area of a circle

156
06:59.170 --> 07:01.300
or the circumference of a circle,

157
07:01.300 --> 07:03.560
and it also can't be written as a fraction.

158
07:03.560 --> 07:07.480
So, there's numbers over here in this irrational realm

159
07:07.480 --> 07:09.830
that we'll touch on later, but as you can see,

160
07:09.830 --> 07:11.670
most of what we're going to deal with

161
07:11.670 --> 07:14.970
is over here on the rational side.

162
07:14.970 --> 07:16.810
And there's really only one more thing you want to remember

163
07:16.810 --> 07:18.500
about these number systems,

164
07:18.500 --> 07:20.580
and that is the symbols that we use

165
07:20.580 --> 07:23.190
to represent these different number systems.

166
07:23.190 --> 07:25.380
So, oftentimes, we'll use the symbol R,

167
07:25.380 --> 07:27.610
and a lot of times we write it like this

168
07:27.610 --> 07:31.100
with this little double line as the side of the R.

169
07:31.100 --> 07:34.590
So, we'll represent real numbers with the letter R

170
07:34.590 --> 07:37.240
We'll represent rational numbers with the letter Q,

171
07:37.240 --> 07:41.950
and again, oftentimes we put this little second line on it.

172
07:41.950 --> 07:45.900
Integers we often name with Z.

173
07:45.900 --> 07:50.420
Whole numbers, and this one's easier, we use W,

174
07:50.420 --> 07:54.940
and natural numbers we oftentimes use N.

175
07:54.940 --> 07:56.730
Irrational numbers, strangely enough,

176
07:56.730 --> 07:58.950
doesn't really have its own symbol,

177
07:58.950 --> 08:02.470
but what we'll often say for irrational numbers,

178
08:02.470 --> 08:05.940
because irrational numbers is all of the real numbers

179
08:05.940 --> 08:08.980
except all of the rational numbers

180
08:08.980 --> 08:11.340
and everything included in rational numbers,

181
08:11.340 --> 08:13.810
we'll express this like this.

182
08:13.810 --> 08:16.020
We'll say all the real numbers

183
08:16.020 --> 08:20.620
minus all of the rational numbers.

184
08:20.620 --> 08:22.300
Or you can really think about this as

185
08:22.300 --> 08:26.130
all the real numbers except all the rational numbers.

186
08:26.130 --> 08:28.230
So, irrational numbers is just really

187
08:28.230 --> 08:31.750
everything else outside of this rational realm,

188
08:31.750 --> 08:33.740
and so we often express it that way.

189
08:33.740 --> 08:35.630
And then the last thing is that,

190
08:35.630 --> 08:38.660
keep in mind, we've been talking about these number sets,

191
08:38.660 --> 08:40.930
all these different sets of natural numbers,

192
08:40.930 --> 08:43.090
whole numbers, irrational numbers.

193
08:43.090 --> 08:45.410
These are all just different number sets,

194
08:45.410 --> 08:47.260
and we can express a number set.

195
08:47.260 --> 08:51.910
Oftentimes when we write a number set we use set notation.

196
08:51.910 --> 08:54.630
And so for instance, you might see something like this.

197
08:54.630 --> 08:59.630
We might say the set of whole numbers is this.

198
08:59.730 --> 09:03.750
We write these curly brackets

199
09:03.750 --> 09:05.340
and then we write out our whole numbers,

200
09:05.340 --> 09:09.680
so zero, one, two, three, four,

201
09:09.680 --> 09:11.200
like this, dot dot dot,

202
09:11.200 --> 09:13.580
and then we close our curly braces.

203
09:13.580 --> 09:14.830
This is set notation,

204
09:14.830 --> 09:17.470
and this says the set of whole numbers

205
09:17.470 --> 09:19.170
includes all of these things,

206
09:19.170 --> 09:22.340
and you list all of the items that are included in the set

207
09:22.340 --> 09:24.320
inside these curly braces.

208
09:24.320 --> 09:27.400
But, we can also use set notation to list,

209
09:27.400 --> 09:29.730
really, anything else that we want.

210
09:29.730 --> 09:34.040
For example, I might choose to list out a set of...

211
09:34.040 --> 09:37.333
So, I'm going to say taco ingredients,

212
09:39.350 --> 09:44.350
and I'm going to say that that includes in that set is,

213
09:44.470 --> 09:48.330
let's say lettuce,

214
09:48.330 --> 09:50.610
let's say meat,

215
09:50.610 --> 09:53.830
let's say cheese,

216
09:53.830 --> 09:55.743
maybe salsa,

217
09:56.860 --> 09:58.890
maybe sour cream,

218
09:58.890 --> 10:01.740
and I could keep going

219
10:01.740 --> 10:02.940
and tell you

220
10:03.820 --> 10:04.780
what's in

221
10:06.070 --> 10:08.520
this set of taco ingredients.

222
10:08.520 --> 10:11.660
So, if I'm serving tacos and I want to write out

223
10:11.660 --> 10:14.540
all of the taco ingredients that I want to prepare,

224
10:14.540 --> 10:17.860
I could, if I wanted to, put that in set notation.

225
10:17.860 --> 10:19.310
I would write it out like this,

226
10:19.310 --> 10:22.540
and just make sure to include everything that I'm serving

227
10:22.540 --> 10:23.890
at my taco night.

228
10:23.890 --> 10:25.610
So, you can use set notation

229
10:25.610 --> 10:28.390
not only for these kinds of number sets

230
10:28.390 --> 10:32.100
but for anything else in math that sort of makes up a set

231
10:32.100 --> 10:34.210
or for anything in real life like this

232
10:34.210 --> 10:35.880
that isn't even mathematical

233
10:35.880 --> 10:40.040
but is just a list of items that you want to gather together

234
10:40.040 --> 10:41.113
as part of a set.